founder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_08", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "The group's neighbourhood turnover peaks at exactly 1.0 at t=59304, and a direct \u00b15000 snapshot diff there yields Jaccard = 0.0: all 115 prior contact edges are dropped and 99 entirely new ones appear. The old partner cohort (2, 61, 63, 116, 239, 918, 926, 1110, 1792, 1922) goes nearly silent afterwards while the new cohort (40, 80, 184, 250, 327, 493, 515, 785, 897, 924, 1348, 1505, 1750) was nearly absent beforehand, so neither prior popularity nor time-ignoring connectivity explains it. Comparable \u00b15000 diffs earlier (J=0.53) and later within the new regime (J=0.33, reshuffling among the same new pool) show ordinary gradual churn, and event volume is flat or slightly lower across the transition, ruling out a volume burst. This is an abrupt regime switch of interaction partners."}, "raw_final_response": "**Findings:**\n\n- **Sharp change point at t\u224859304**: turnover rises from ~0.44 baseline to **1.0** (complete replacement) and stays at 1.0 for ~1500 units, then decays back \u2014 a single localized transition, not a flat gradual trend (step_02).\n- **Zero overlap across the change point**: with \u00b15000 half-windows, 115 edges in the \"before\" set, 99 in the \"after\" set, **Jaccard = 0.0** \u2014 every contact was replaced (step_03).\n- **Baseline turnover is gradual**: an equivalent \u00b15000 comparison earlier in the period (45074\u201350073 vs 50074\u201355074) gives Jaccard 0.534 with only 37 removals (step_05); a later comparison inside the new regime gives 0.333 with reshuffling *within the same new partner pool* (step_09). So 0.0 is anomalous, not the norm.\n- **Confounder controls**: the old partners (2, 61, 63, 116, 239, 918, 926, 1110, 1792, 1922) essentially vanish after the change point (0\u20134 events each over 15k units; step_06), and the new partners (40, 80, 184, 250, 327, 493, 515, 785, 897, 924, 1348, 1505, 1750) were near-absent before it (0\u20135 events; step_07) \u2014 so this is not explained by pre-existing popularity or by a static connection ignoring time. Volume is flat-to-slightly-lower across the transition (155 vs 124 events; 207/199 at the period edges; step_04, step_08), so no activity burst drives the effect.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_107b45e5\",\n  \"decision\": \"present\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [114, 140, 182, 293, 356, 654, 731, 959, 1107, 1260, 1268, 1311],\n  \"window\": [54304, 64304],\n  \"confidence\": 0.91,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"The group's neighbourhood turnover peaks at exactly 1.0 at t=59304, and a direct \u00b15000 snapshot diff there yields Jaccard = 0.0: all 115 prior contact edges are dropped and 99 entirely new ones appear. The old partner cohort (2, 61, 63, 116, 239, 918, 926, 1110, 1792, 1922) goes nearly silent afterwards while the new cohort (40, 80, 184, 250, 327, 493, 515, 785, 897, 924, 1348, 1505, 1750) was nearly absent beforehand, so neither prior popularity nor time-ignoring connectivity explains it. Comparable \u00b15000 diffs earlier (J=0.53) and later within the new regime (J=0.33, reshuffling among the same new pool) show ordinary gradual churn, and event volume is flat or slightly lower across the transition, ruling out a volume burst. This is an abrupt regime switch of interaction partners.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 42670, "total_output_tokens": 3333, "total_tokens": 